destructive_patterns.explanation:
DELETE without WHERE removes ALL rows from the table:

\ - Each row deletion is logged in binary log \ - CAN be rolled back if within a transaction \ - Fires DELETE triggers for each row \ - Does NOT reset AUTO_INCREMENT counter \ - Much slower than TRUNCATE for large tables

\ If you meant to delete all rows: \ - TRUNCATE is faster (but non-transactional) \ - DELETE in transaction allows rollback

\ Otherwise, add a WHERE clause: \ DELETE FROM tablename WHERE condition;

\ Preview what would be deleted: \ SELECT COUNT(*) FROM tablename; -- all rows! \ SELECT * FROM tablename LIMIT 10;
